// `usize` looks like a primitive and is what `sizeof` itself RETURNS, which makes it the trap in M6's rule.
// It maps to C `size_t`, whose width is a property of the target, not of the language: 4 bytes on wasm32
// and thumbv6m, 8 on x86_64/aarch64. Folding it would bake the host's answer into a cross-compiled binary.
//
// This is the line M6 draws: kama folds what the LANGUAGE fixes (a width in the type's name, resting on
// premises kama_runtime.h asserts) and never what the ABI decides. `isize` is rejected for the same reason.
fn int32 idN<const N: int32>() { return N; }

fn int32 main() { return idN::<(sizeof(usize))>(); }
